    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's domestic radio and television system centers on Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting, or IRIB. Understanding it requires separating constitutional governance, different programming services, international broadcasting, and independently documented concerns about editorial practice.",
    "tags": ["Broadcasting", "IRIB", "Radio", "Television", "Media governance", "International broadcasting"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:14:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:14:51Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Radio and Television in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand IRIB's constitutional governance, international broadcasting, entertainment services, and documented questions about editorial practice."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "institution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An institution and a range of services",
        "content": "Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting, usually abbreviated IRIB, is the institution at the center of Iran's domestic broadcasting system. The independent rights organizations FIDH and Justice for Iran describe its monopoly over domestic radio and television in their 2020 investigation. That institutional concentration should be distinguished from the diversity of formats carried by broadcasting: news, drama, interviews, documentaries, and other programming are different editorial products even when they share an organizational framework. [s2]\n\nInternational services add another distinction. Press TV addresses English-speaking audiences through news and documentary work, while iFilm's English-language website presents series, films, and cultural programming. An account of Iranian broadcasting therefore needs to ask both who controls a service and whom its programming is intended to reach. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "governance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Formal authority",
        "content": "Article 175 of the constitution assigns appointment and dismissal of the broadcasting organization's head to the Supreme Leader. It separately provides for supervision by a council with representatives of the President, the judiciary head, and parliament, the Islamic Consultative Assembly. Appointment authority and supervision are distinct relationships; the constitutional text does not make the council the appointing body. [s1]\n\nThe same article qualifies its guarantee of expression by reference to Islamic criteria and the country's interests. This describes the formal framework. It does not, by itself, demonstrate how competing views are represented in broadcasts or how effectively supervision operates. Those questions require evidence about actual decisions and programming. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "governance-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Appointment and supervision under Article 175",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  L[\"Supreme Leader\"] -->|\"Appoints and dismisses\"| H[\"Broadcasting head\"]\n  P[\"President\"] -->|\"Two representatives\"| C[\"Supervisory council\"]\n  J[\"Head of judiciary\"] -->|\"Two representatives\"| C\n  M[\"Parliament\"] -->|\"Two representatives\"| C\n  C -->|\"Supervises functioning\"| B[\"Broadcasting organization\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates the Leader's appointment power from the council's supervisory function. It represents the constitutional text, rather than a finding about effective editorial accountability.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "international",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Broadcasting beyond a domestic audience",
        "content": "Press TV describes itself as an international English-language news network, with newsroom, website, program, and documentary functions. Its institutional account is evidence of how the broadcaster presents its purpose, rather than independent proof of accuracy or balance. Its own materials give differing launch formulations: the website refers to June 2007, while a broadcaster brochure identifies July 2, 2007. The defensible shared conclusion is that the service began in 2007. [s3][s5]\n\nInternational broadcasting also includes entertainment. The inspected iFilm website separates series, movies, shows, schedules, and reception information. Its cultural sections include arts, music, and tourism. This provides a useful counterpoint to treating every international broadcast service as a continuous news channel. Language adaptation and program selection can introduce audiences to Iranian screen production, although the existence of an English website cannot establish who watches or what viewers think. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Evaluating practice and preserving evidence",
        "content": "FIDH and Justice for Iran's 2020 report investigated coerced confessions and defamatory broadcasts. Its research combined program analysis, case material, judicial documents, and interviews with victims. The authors also acknowledged obstacles including inaccessible official archives and restricted access to people inside Iran. These findings concern documented broadcasting practices; they should neither be reduced to an institutional mission statement nor generalized into a description of every entertainment program. [s2]\n\nA separate primary record is the US Treasury's November 2022 announcement of sanctions against senior IRIB employees, explicitly linked to allegations concerning forced confessions. That document establishes the US government's action and stated rationale. Independent case research remains necessary when assessing the underlying conduct. [s6]\n\nFor readers, the practical distinction is between a broadcast's existence and the truth of its contents. A recording can establish what was transmitted, but an interview conducted under detention requires scrutiny of consent and circumstances. Likewise, an archived schedule establishes planned programming rather than actual audience reach. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4", "s6"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
